Z||000006||GeeDee86GT||12-13-2000||10:38 PM||||Websites are great fun, but addictive. I'm and old fart and no hi-techer. I used a freeware editor Max's HTML Beauty at <A HREF="http://www.max.co.yu/htmlbeauty/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.max.co.yu/htmlbeauty/</A>   It's pretty easy to use.  My page is at Geocities as well <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/garby_d</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d/siggy.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||Songman||12-13-2000||10:44 PM||||Definitely go for Dreamweaver! FrontPage is fine but it is only fully compatible with IE. That means none of your stuff will work in Netscape and since the majority of people still use Netscape that is not a good thing. <P>When I was using FrontPage 2000, I would design my page with it and then go back and edit the html by hand until everything worked. That is just too much trouble and if you don't know html it is pretty close to impossible.<P>Dreamweaver is cross platform and very easy to learn. I have been using version 3 and just now upgraded to 4. <P>As a matter of fact, since we are all friends here in Fiero land, send me an e-mail and I will tell you how to get it...(free) Shhhh...||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||12-14-2000||02:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>since the majority of people still use Netscape that is not a good thing. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>since when??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I haven't used netscrape in years, and now I can't stand it... of course I haven't tried the latest version.. but anyway, I'd be interested in aquiring Dreamweaver 4 as well...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||6
Z||000009||onfire||12-14-2000||04:03 AM||||My website is hosted by <A HREF="http://www.deja.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.deja.com/</A>  <BR>and they have their own editor that pops up when you want to edit your site. Its pretty basic, and easy to use. Plus you can fine tune your website with your own html code.I can't wait to get another Fiero. It will give me some incentive to put something useful on my site.<P>------------------<BR>"I seen it on the internet, and I believe everthing on the net."<BR><A HREF="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/on_fire.html" TARGET=_blank>Check out my waist of banbwidth.</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick's Dad||12-14-2000||10:18 AM||||I run my company's site (http://www.cameraco.com) on FP2K. I test in Netscape (I even, occasionally, check it in WebTV). Because it is a commerce site, I keep it clean, graphically simple and quick loading.<P>Spend some time thinking about what you want your site to look like, then most any HTML editor will take care of the rest, as long as you learn how to use it. Because of the reasons stated above, I rarely use the extras. Since the company paid for it, I didn't have to warry about that end of it....<P>My site, on the other hand (http://www.starhero.com ...not active, yet), will be little more elaborate, and I may get into learning Dreamweaver. One thing I am waiting for, is I'm still mucking with Newtek's Inspire 3D, as I'm trying to build a 3D model (actually, right now I'd be happy with 2D) of something from the story I'm writing....<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Black88GT||12-14-2000||10:41 AM||||I'll have to check out that Dreamweaver software.  My programming knowledge is limited, right now I am doing C++ but I have had some experience with VB.  Do you have to know HTML for the dreamweaver program?<P><BR>thanks<P>P.S. lisa mine is the geocities page 3487 (motorcities)<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000012||DaRkLoRD||12-14-2000||02:24 PM||||knowing HTML code is useful for tweaking the page, but Dreamweaver is essentially a "drag & drop" application.. you actually place the text and images on the page, and it appears in a browser almost exactly the same as on the screen.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000013||afRaceR||12-14-2000||07:33 PM||||Black88GT....if you can learn C++ you can definately learn HTML. <BR>I used to be pretty good with C++, visual C++, pascal, and basic, and HTML is the easiest by far. There are really only a few basic commands.<BR>There are many sites on the web that explain the basics, once you understand that you can view the html code of any page on the net through your browser and see how all of it gets compiled.<BR>Knowing other program languages is also a plus because you can do more advanced pages with the other languages.<BR>You can also do what Songman does and use the HTML programs to edit the basic site and then code the HTML in to fine tune everything.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||p4n1c||12-14-2000||10:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>and since the majority of people still use Netscape that is not a good thing.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree that Frontpage just doesn't cut it for compatibility and well anyone would be best to steer clear of it, but a large number of people are actually moving away from Netscape unless they are in a Unix type environment(read: only thing availible next to the pay for use Opera and text-based Lynx) and no, not because IE is intergrated or Microsoft is assimilating them.<P>The truth of the matter is that Netscape is dieing a very slow death.  Mozilla is still strong in the open source community, but Netscape itself is degrading rapidly.  With the latest release of Netscape 6, many, many people have been complaining of bugs that could be picked up easily by the testers.  The number of people unhappy with the product is astounding.  <P>Netscape is major bloatware and I've found that it does not display validated HTML 4 pages properly, like it doesn't display a centered table, it'll move it off to the left, etc.  I used Netscape 6 b&lt;something&gt; on Linux and all it did was crash, which was another complaint of the release final of Netscape 6.<P>I used to be a Netscape advocate and user, but I've long since switched(after using 4) to IE(starting with IE 4.01, anything before was worse then Netscape). IE 5.5 seems to be rock solid, for myself anyway, I've yet to have it crash on me, now if they'd just port it to Linux, Solaris got it, why can't Linux? =]<P>And before you ask, no I am not a Microsoft supporter, far from it, I'm a Linux, BeOS advocate.  But beyond the bad guy demeanor in the exec situation, some programmers in Microsoft are actually well respected in the community(Michael Abrash(designer of the quake engine, wrote many books including the Black Art of 3D programming)).  We won't get into the Windows programmers though. =]  Actually moving away from the on-top of DOS approach was the best thing they could ever do, and moving into the more stable NT platform(Which was designed by the guy who did the design work for VMS(VMS + 1 = WNT)) was a good choice to get away from the nasty 16-bit compatibility code which was mainly the cause for many headaches while using the OS.<P>Best compatibility?  Grab an HTML reference, bookmark w3c's validator and code 'er out by hand.<P>Next best, dreamweaver. =]||||reg||1
Z||000015||tgowens||12-15-2000||03:35 AM||||well, since you asked........Dreamweaver is a good program but, I use Photoshop and hard HTML and JavaScript coding mostly for the sites I do -- feel free to look at any of them........my Fiero site is  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>  - I have done the sites for Classic Fiero Owners Group <A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A>  as well as Golden Gate Fieros  <A HREF="http://www.goldengatefieros.org" TARGET=_blank>www.goldengatefieros.org</A>  - My graphics site is West Highland Graphics & Design  <A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>www.whgraphics.com</A> <P>Lisa, check out the links page on my site  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||tgowens||12-15-2000||04:00 AM||||oh yes, I code for cross-browser functionality too even though the stats from the sites I host show that the majority use Netscape.......||||reg||1
Z||000017||Songman||12-15-2000||08:58 AM||||Personally, I use Netscape and IE. I have always liked Netscape better but I admit that Netscape is falling behind in functionality and stability. The newer versions of Netscape tend to crash more often than the older versions and sometimes even freeze so a restart is needed. But, of all the sites that I do, the stats show that Netscape is still well in the majority. <P>I started my career as a graphic designer on a Mac way back in 1985 so I have been using Photoshop and other such programs since version 1.0. And it is still very much needed to do a good website. And now that I use a PC I am glad that most of those programs have crossed platforms.<P>My main programs that I use to take a website from start to finish are Photoshop, Freehand, Dreamweaver, Imagestyler, ImageReady, and sometimes others. It is not a one program trick to make a good website in my opinion. I have Flash and all of the other 'goodies' programs like that but don't really use them for my sites. With my cable modem they are fine but most people still have slow internet connections and I feel like they don't want to wait through the fluff to get to the meat of the site.<P>Anyway... the offer is still out there. If you want info on getting any of these programs, e-mail me. I don't want to post this publicly, but I will say that first you need to go to <A HREF="http://www.bigredh.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bigredh.com</A>  and download the client software. When you get that installed, I will tell you privately what to do after that...<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||p4n1c||12-15-2000||03:43 PM||||Personally I think all web browsers don't cut it.  They try to implement to many external and non-standard features instead of focusing on displaying a page according to the standards and going through the right channels to bring in a new feature, but everyone loves to be proprietary.  <P>It would make web designers lives easier if the browsers would adhere to the HTML standard for displaying pages.  I have no quarrel with plugins like flash, fireworks, etc.  I think pages made with those look great.  But it would look even better if the damn web browsers would adhere to HTML standards and it would look the same everywhere.<P>Oh and according to: <A HREF="http://browserwatch.internet.com/stats/stats.html" TARGET=_blank>http://browserwatch.internet.com/stats/stats.html</A>  <P>IE is winning the majority with 61%. Netscape is at 21%.  Not a lot of people are using opera.  Which is at around 3%.<P>But it all depends on the site people are visiting.  Not one could tell you the real story behind who's using what and it also goes along the line of to each his own.  But you'll find because of lack of features and the lack of stability that Netscape numbers will start dropping more and more and people will be either moving to IE or Opera(it will be freely released(with limitations), next year).<P>Sorry if this all sounds like a rant or trying to start a browser war, I'm just bored and like talking about things like this when I'm not talking about cars. =]<P>Take it easy,<BR>p4n1c||||reg||1
Z||000019||DaRkLoRD||12-15-2000||05:20 PM||||I'm starting to think that not a lot of software is "good" these days.. certainly none of the web browsers.. I honestly can't remember the last major application that I got that I thoroughly enjoyed using, and didn't consider to be unstable, bloated, and poorly designed. Probably Photoshop.. it doesn't have a lot of useless extras built in, and once you know your way around, it's very intuitive to use.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000020||Gonzo_442||12-15-2000||06:55 PM||||It looks like the popular vote is going to Dreamweaver.<P>However, there is always one little guy in the back who argues the point. Looks like this time it's me.<P>I am in the process of moving my site from one server to another (so it is currently down  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">). However, i like to get my hands dirty and do it the old fashhioned way. Therefore, i recommend BBEdit by <A HREF="http://www.barebones.com/" TARGET=_blank>BareBones Software</A>. Of course one of the requirements for this is a good computer (a Mac  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) but you didn't specify.<P>As for professional results, imo, hand code is the best way to do it.<P>And along the theme of software discussions... p4n1c is right, netscape is dying. But, it is a better thing to test with than explorer. Some people are willing to put up with bloated software full of bugs and other nightmares. Others like to do something about it, people like the guys at <A HREF="http://www.icab.de" TARGET=_blank>iCab</A>.<P>Keep your site simple and clean, it's the best way to make it useful/helpful. And, always, design first, then create it. Having a site with everything is useless if it isn't organized. Make a plan (at least in your head, on paper is better) and follow it. Don't let your site wander. Good luck, and don't forget to post a link when you're done.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>--<BR>g||||reg||1
Z||000021||DaRkLoRD||12-15-2000||08:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Gonzo_442:<BR><B>Don't let your site wander.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmm.. one of the things I tend to do with my website, is adding whatever I want, whenever I want.. especially Photoshop Tutorials.<P>but having a plan is good... if you have a set number of goals for the site, it'll help keep it from being perpetually "under construction", as so many sites are...<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000022||theogre||12-15-2000||11:39 PM||||I use Word for allot of mine. I use Notepad for fine tuning and editing pages with Java Script in them like my TOC frame in tunnel 6.<P>I've used word 95 with the internet add on. Word 97, and now Word 2000. It's hard to beat the size of the dictionary in Office. It works well for most stuff but if you aren't real carefull with Word 2K or Forntpage you can end up with really huge pages fast.<P>I also use allot of stuf from Cnet's Builder.com, Webmonkey, and ZDnet. These places have all sorts of free things that actually work. I used ZDnet's icon editor to create the little icon you get if you add the my Fiero Cave to your IE favorites. (Try drawing a recogizable object in a 16x16 square.)<P>There's also a photo editor called GIMP that I'll be checking out this week. <A HREF="http://www.gimp.org" TARGET=_blank>www.gimp.org</A>  It looks interesting. Don't know much about it yet.||||reg||1
